# Book Writing Guidelines for RustNES Emulator

I want to write a book on how to build a NES emulator in rust.

In order to write an easy to follow book, I want to follow an incremental approach similar to https://craftinginterpreters.com/contents.html, and a test-driven approach similar to the http://raytracerchallenge.com/

The book chapters should be written in markdown, so that it can easily be converted in a web app.

## Target Audience

The book is written for:
* People interested in emulators and how they work
* Readers with some experience in Rust (but not necessarily experts)
* Anyone fascinated by retro gaming and NES architecture
* Developers who want to learn through building a complex project

## Multi-Track Structure

The book follows four main tracks as outlined in PLAN.md:
* **Track 1 [T1]**: Pixel Display - Getting basic components working to display a single pixel
* **Track 2 [T2]**: Pattern & Sprite Rendering - Adding tiles, sprites and animation
* **Track 3 [T3]**: Interactive Graphics - Implementing backgrounds, scrolling and input
* **Track 4 [T4]**: Complete NES - Adding audio, all mappers, and full game compatibility

The reader can follow one of two paths:
* Read the book from start to end, experiencing the incremental development process
* Follow a specific track (T1, T2, T3, or T4) as their interest/time permits

## Chapter Structure

Each chapter should be structured to support the multi-track approach:
* Begin with essential T1 content that all readers need
* Clearly mark track-specific sections with indicators [T1], [T2], [T3], [T4]
* Include "Track Milestone" checkpoints with self-assessment questions
* Provide "Fast Forward" sections at milestone points for track-specific readers
* End chapters with "What You've Built" summaries including screenshots/examples

## Content Presentation Guidelines

### Code Presentation
* Follow test-first approach: show test code, then implementation
* Include complete code for critical components
* For longer implementations, show key sections with "[...]" for omitted code
* Explain the "why" behind implementation decisions, not just the "how"
* Highlight differences when extending previous track code

### Theory and Practice Balance
* Limit theoretical sections to 2-3 pages before showing code
* Follow "just-in-time" learning - introduce concepts right before they're needed
* Use diagrams liberally to illustrate NES architecture concepts
* Balance historical accuracy with practical implementation

### Engagement Techniques
* Include "NES History" sidebars for interesting historical context
* Add "Emulation Insight" boxes explaining why certain approaches were chosen
* Use "Common Pitfall" warnings to help readers avoid debugging headaches
* Add humor through relevant game references and programming jokes
* Pose thought questions that encourage readers to consider alternatives
* Include "Challenge" sections for readers who want to explore beyond main content

### Visual Elements
* Use consistent color coding for different tracks
* Include progress indicators showing where each chapter fits in the overall project
* Use screenshots and visual comparisons between expected and actual output
* Create diagrams for memory layouts, CPU processes, and PPU rendering

### Rust-Specific Content
* Explain Rust-specific concepts briefly when first used
* Highlight idiomatic Rust patterns relevant to emulator development
* Show both basic and optimized Rust implementations where appropriate
* Include notes on performance considerations specific to Rust

## Testing Framework
* Establish a consistent testing methodology used throughout the book
* Include test ROMs with expected outputs for each milestone
* Explain how to verify results at each stage
* Show debugging techniques when tests fail

## Writing Style

The writing style should be:
* Conversational and approachable, with occasional humor
* Technical but not overly academic
* Encouraging and supportive of the reader's journey
* Focused on practical understanding rather than exhaustive detail
* Engaging enough to make a complex technical subject enjoyable

## Book Organization

The book is organized in:
* Initial chapters that describe the NES architecture and project setup
* Core implementation chapters following the multi-track approach
* Appendix sections covering UI, debugging tools, WASM integration, etc.

Remember: This is not a reference book with strict rules to be followed on each chapter/section. It should be read with pleasure, with readers discovering new aspects of emulation as they progress through their chosen track(s).